=== Introduction
--- Creation Out of Nothing [title]
overline: IFB · KJVO · Sunday Morning Class
heading: Creation Out of Nothing
sub: Defending the Biblical Doctrine of *Ex Nihilo* and Answering the Divine Council Theory of Michael Heiser
note: Scripture Alone Settles the Question

--- The Question Before Us
kicker: Why This Matters
- Historic Christian doctrine holds that God created all things out of nothing (*ex nihilo*), by His word alone.
- A popular academic theory, associated with the late Michael Heiser and the “Divine Council Worldview”, claims the Old Testament originally taught a council of spirit-beings, a “pantheon,” ruling alongside God, and reads a watery chaos as pre-existing His act of creating.
- This is not a harmless footnote. It quietly relocates God’s unique, incommunicable glory as Creator onto other beings.
- We will test the theory against its own proof texts, then set the whole matter under the plain testimony of the New Testament and the grammar of Genesis.

=== The New Testament testimony
--- John 1:1–3
kicker: Text One
sub: The Pre-Existence of the Word
scripture: John 1:1-3 | mark: without him was not any thing made that was made
panel: The Defense | “Not any thing” made without Him leaves no crack for pre-existing matter, chaos, or a council of co-workers. Every category of created thing, spirit or matter, passes through the Word, and the Word passes through no one.

--- Colossians 1:16–17
kicker: Text Two
sub: The Scope of Christ’s Creation
scripture: Colossians 1:16-17 | mark: thrones, or dominions, or principalities, or powers; he is before all things
panel: The Defense | Paul names the very categories a divine-council reading depends on, thrones, dominions, principalities, powers, and puts every one of them on the created side of the ledger. If the “council” is made by Christ, it cannot have stood alongside Him at creation. He alone is “before all things.”

--- Hebrews 11:3
kicker: Text Three
sub: The Origin of Visible Matter
scripture: Hebrews 11:3 | mark: the worlds were framed by the word of God
panel: The Defense | This is the New Testament’s own definition of *ex nihilo*. The visible universe was not organized out of some pre-existing, visible material, a chaotic deep sitting there before God got to work. It came strictly from His spoken word.

--- Romans 4:17
kicker: Text Four
sub: The Sovereign Power of God
scripture: Romans 4:17 | mark: calleth those things which be not as though they were
panel: The Defense | God’s creative power is described as calling what does not exist into existence, not reshaping, not organizing, not commissioning help from beings already on hand. “Things which be not” is as plain a denial of pre-existing raw material as Scripture offers.

--- Revelation 4:11
kicker: Text Five
sub: The Ultimate Purpose
scripture: Revelation 4:11 | mark: thou hast created all things
panel: The Defense | The heavenly host itself, the very beings a council theory would promote to co-creators, falls down to say the glory belongs to God alone. If they knew of colleagues in creation, this would be the moment to name them. They don’t.

--- Isaiah 44:24
kicker: Text Six
sub: “By Myself”: God’s Own Claim
scripture: Isaiah 44:24 | mark: stretcheth forth the heavens alone; spreadeth abroad the earth by myself
panel: The Defense | God does not merely say He made all things. He specifies the manner: “alone,” “by myself.” This is not a passage that leaves room for silent partners. Any theory that populates creation week with a working council must first explain past this verse, not around it.

=== Answering the proof texts
--- Job 38:4–7
kicker: Answering a Proof Text
sub: The Foundation Is Christ
scripture: Job 38:4-7 | mark: all the sons of God shouted for joy
panel: The Defense | Bible-believers hold more than one view of who the “sons of God” are here, some angels, others the redeemed line from Seth onward, and that is a secondary question, not the point at issue tonight. What matters for our purpose is the foundation itself. Scripture names a foundation deeper than day one’s dirt: Christ Himself, the cornerstone laid before the world began ([[1 Corinthians 3:11]]; [[1 Peter 1:20]]), the very foundation Abraham himself saw by faith and rejoiced in ([[John 8:56]]). On either view of the “sons,” this text gives no committee of co-creators. It gives worshippers glad for a foundation God alone laid in Christ.

--- Psalm 82:1, 6–7
kicker: Answering a Proof Text
sub: The “Gods” Who Die Like Men
scripture: Psalm 82:1; Psalm 82:6-7 | mark: ye shall die like men
panel: The Defense | This is the Divine Council Worldview’s cornerstone text, and it refutes itself. Whatever these “gods” are, the Psalm’s own verdict is that they will *die like men*. An eternal pantheon does not die. Christ Himself applies this psalm to those “unto whom the word of God came” ([[John 10:34-36]]), corrupt human judges under divine indictment, not a co-eternal spirit council.

--- Deuteronomy 32:8–9
kicker: Answering a Proof Text
sub: A Textual Argument, Not a Scriptural One
scripture: Deuteronomy 32:8-9 | mark: the Most High divided to the nations their inheritance
panel: The Defense | The Divine Council argument here rests on preferring a variant reading, “sons of God,” found in some Dead Sea fragments and the Greek Septuagint, over the Hebrew Masoretic Text the King James translators followed under God’s providential preservation. We do not rebuild doctrine on disputed fragments against the received, settled text. And even granting that angelic princes are set over nations elsewhere in Scripture ([[Daniel 10:13]]; [[Daniel 10:20-21]]), that describes derived, creaturely authority under God’s appointment, never a council sharing in His work of creation.

=== A question of method
--- Reading Scripture Through Canaanite Eyes
kicker: A Question of Method
panel: The Claim | Much of the Divine Council Worldview is built by importing Ugaritic and Canaanite mythology, the Baal Cycle, the god El and his council, as the lens for reading Hebrew words like “council,” “sons of God,” and “Most High,” on the assumption that Israel’s writers shared their pagan neighbors’ cosmology.
panel: The Support | Scripture is its own interpreter and needs no pagan mythology to explain what it means ([[2 Peter 1:20-21]]; [[2 Timothy 3:16-17]]). And where the Bible does speak of the “gods” of the nations, it does not describe them as junior members of a real heavenly bureaucracy. It calls them nothing: “all the gods of the nations are idols” ([[Psalm 96:5]]); Israel’s sacrifices to them were sacrifices “unto devils, and not to God” ([[Deuteronomy 32:17]]; [[1 Corinthians 10:20]]). Building doctrine by reading Canaan’s myths into Moses gets the authority backwards.

=== Foundations in Genesis
--- The Grammar Itself Answers
kicker: Foundations in Genesis
sub: Three grammatical and structural facts that rule out a plural “council” of creators at the very source text.
- The Grammatical Rule of *Elohim*
- The Structure of Genesis 1:1 as an Absolute Beginning
- The Singular Execution in Genesis 1:26–27

--- The Grammatical Rule of Elohim
kicker: Genesis, Point One
p: The Hebrew word *Elohim* (אֱלֹהִים) carries a plural ending (-im). Yet in [[Genesis 1:1]], its verb is *bara* (בָּרָא), strictly singular: “He created.”
panel: The Support | A plural noun paired with a singular verb, in Hebrew, signals majesty or a single entity acting as one, never a voting committee. The King James translators rendered it correctly as the singular “God.” It points to the unified Godhead, Father, Son, and Spirit, acting in absolute unison, and rules out a plural council of separate gods watching or helping Him work.

--- The Structure of Genesis 1:1
kicker: Genesis, Point Two
sub: An Absolute Beginning
panel-warn: The Claim | Heiser and other academics argue Genesis 1:1 is a dependent clause, “When God began to create... the earth was a watery chaos,” which requires reading the first word, *Bereshit* (בְּרֵאשִׁית), as “in the beginning of.”
panel: The Support | The traditional, received reading is the independent, absolute form: “In the beginning...” a foundational announcement of an absolute start. Verse 1 is the completed creation of everything from nothing; verse 2 simply zooms in to describe the earth’s initial unformed state before the six days of forming and filling begin. No pre-existing chaos is required, only a text willing to stand as it was handed down.

--- Genesis 1:26–27
kicker: Genesis, Point Three
sub: The Singular Execution
scripture: Genesis 1:26-27 | mark: Let us make man in our image; So God created man in his own image
panel: The Support | The consultation of verse 26 uses the plural “Us,” the fellowship within the Trinity itself. But the execution in verse 27 drops to strictly singular pronouns: *his, he, he*. A council did not create mankind; the singular Godhead did. Angels never share the image or the creative power of God.

=== Summary
--- What the Theory Gets Wrong
kicker: Sorting the Claims
table: The Overreach | The Actual Testimony
tr: That a heavenly host implies co-creators. | Angels are creatures who worship, not architects who build ([[Job 38:4-7]]; [[Revelation 4:11]]).
tr: That “gods” in Psalm 82 means an eternal pantheon. | Scripture’s own “gods” die like men ([[Psalm 82:7]]), no eternal rivals to the Most High.
tr: That a disputed textual variant should override the received Hebrew text. | God made all things “alone” and “by myself” ([[Isaiah 44:24]]), a claim no council can share.

--- Scripture Speaks Clearly
kicker: Summary
xr: John 1:1-3 | Nothing was made without the Word.
xr: Colossians 1:16-17 | Christ is before all things, visible and invisible.
xr: Hebrews 11:3 | The seen was not made of things which appear.
xr: Romans 4:17 | God calls things which be not as though they were.
xr: Revelation 4:11 | All things were created for His pleasure.
xr: Isaiah 44:24 | God stretched forth the heavens alone, by Himself.
xr: Job 38:4-7 | Whoever the “sons” are, the foundation is Christ, not a council’s handiwork.
xr: Psalm 82:6-7 | Scripture’s own “gods” die like men; Christ applies it to earthly leaders.
xr: 2 Peter 1:20-21 | Method: Canaanite mythology is not the lens Scripture hands us to read itself.
xr: Genesis 1:1 | Singular verbs, an absolute beginning, and singular execution.

=== Conclusion
--- One God, One Word, One Work
kicker: Conclusion
lead: The Bible consistently presents one sovereign God who created all things out of nothing by the power of His word. There is no eternal matter, no pre-existing chaos that limited Him, and no council of lesser gods that shares His creative work, however loudly modern scholarship dresses the claim in Hebrew grammar and ancient Near Eastern parallels.
big: Revelation 4:11 | mark: thou hast created all things

--- Soli Deo Gloria [title]
heading: Soli Deo Gloria
sub: To God alone be the glory for the work of creation.
